part.text:
Further items to confirm:

1. filter active=maybe → all users with active=false match (converter's default FALSE).
2. output:only= (empty) → full entity.
3. PUT with created/modified/userPermissions included in the body → 200 (ignored properties).
4. DELETE of a user referenced by userpermissions → 404 (FK violation), user remains.
5. POST of appearance with properties {"x":null} → **201**!? NPE didn't occur! convertToDatabaseColumn(null) → attribute.toString() → NPE... but 201 was returned. Hmm — either the null value got filtered out earlier, or the converter isn't being called for null (does Hibernate skip converter application for null attribute values? AttributeConverter doesn't get applied to null — Hibernate converts null directly to null!). Exactly: JPA spec — converter isn't called for null; null gets stored. Confirm DB: the row in layerappearances_properties is (id,'x',NULL)? Probably value is NULL. And when read back: convertToEntityAttribute(null)?? On read Hibernate skips converter for null column → map value is null → serialized as "x": null. OK: null in properties → stored as NULL, read back as null. Implementation: store NULL, read as JVal::Null.
6. foo user (ROLE_FOO only) → **403 on all /rest** (URL level) ✓ (both POST and DELETE).
7. Non-ASCII: raw UTF-8 byte sequences (é = 303 251, © = 302 251), `"` escaped as \", `<>&` not escaped ✓. That is, Jackson outputs raw UTF-8 and only escapes quotes/backslashes/control characters.

Now — what about control character escaping? Jackson's default escapes: below 0x20 as \uXXXX (hex uppercase), except \b\t\n\f\r are in short form. Also `"` and `\`. That's it. Good.

With this I'm ready to start writing the Rust service. Finally I'll also do a few quick checks on serialization of properties with null values, and the ordering of tileGridResolutions + PUT with an int for a string field, etc. Minor points. Moving on to the build.

Plan the Cargo project. Let's create the skeleton. Write code carefully module by module.

Actually — one more important thing to verify: **Jetty/Spring URL matching**: `/rest/users/3/userGroups` — also `/rest/users/3/userGroups/` with trailing slash. And **`/rest/users/filter` vs `/rest/users/{id}`**: "filter" matches the static pattern first ✓.

Also: **GET /rest/users/3/userGroups/extra** → Spring gives 404. Axum: no route → 404 ✓.

Also — **405 vs 404**: DELETE /rest/users → Spring gives 405 (path exists, method not allowed)? @RequestMapping without method specification... /rest/users has GET+POST mapped; DELETE /rest/users → 405. Axum also gives 405 ✓.

Also — **PUT /rest/users (no id)** → 405 ✓ axum.

Also re-verify **GET /rest/users/filter when there's also a user named "filter"** — irrelevant.

**Important: charset of request body** — UTF-8 assumed.

Time to write code. Structure:

```
rust/Cargo.toml
rust/src/main.rs      — entry: env, pool, router, serve
rust/src/jval.rs      — JSON value enum with raw numeric text + parser + writer (Java-style)
rust/src/model.rs     — entity structs + equality + to_json
rust/src/db.rs        — pool, next_id, helpers
rust/src/load.rs      — entity loading (single/list/filter, full/restricted)
rust/src/deser.rs     — Jackson-style parsing/merging into model
rust/src/write.rs     — insert/update/delete
rust/src/perms.rs     — auth + permissions
rust/src/handlers.rs  — endpoints
rust/src/app.rs       — router
rust/tests/api.rs     — integration tests
```

Key decision: write my own JSON parser preserving raw number tokens. Parser: standard recursive descent over bytes producing JVal:

```rust
pub enum JVal {
    Null,
    Bool(bool),
    Int(i64),          // integer literal (raw text preserved)
    Float(f64),        // decimal/exponent literal (raw preserved)
    NumRaw { .. }?     // hmm
    Str(String),
    Arr(Vec<JVal>),
    Obj(Vec<(String, JVal)>),  // preserve insertion order, allow duplicate keys? Jackson: later wins. Preserve with dedup semantics: later value replaces earlier (for merge) but for property order — first-occurrence position. Simplify: keep Vec, handle overwrite in deser.
}
